Article 55

1. Promotive efforts of basic health services as referred to in Article 54 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at improving health status more optimally.
2. Promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by providing socialization or counseling to the community regarding healthy lifestyle patterns which include:a. the importance of exclusive breast milk for infants;
b. the importance of immunization for toddlers;
c. clean living patterns;
d. the importance of nutritious food; ande. the dangers of drugs and smoking.
3. In addition to promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (2), Regional Government may conduct other activities in the framework of health provision.

Article 56

1. Preventive efforts of basic health services as referred to in Article 54 paragraph (2) letter a are aimed at preventing the occurrence of disease or health problems.
2. Efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by: a. requiring mothers to provide exclusive breastfeeding to infants up to the age of 6 (six) months;
b. provision of free immunization and vitamins to infants and toddlers; c. provision of nutritious food to toddlers from underprivileged families;
d. optimizing the role of posyandu for health services for mothers and toddlers;
e. conducting periodic health examinations;
f. providing sanitation facilities and clean water in every school; and g. providing special breastfeeding facilities or breastfeeding corner rooms in government agencies or business entities.

Article 20

1. In order to prevent acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government:a. conducts socialization regarding the prevention of violence against women and children by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. provides guidelines on handling acts of violence against women and children;
c. involves community participation in recovery and reintegration; d. develops an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women and children;
e. develops family empowerment programs;
f. strengthens the function of community organizations engaged in women's empowerment and child protection; andg. provides public facilities that are comfortable and safe for women and children.
2. Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out by OPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. mental and spiritual;
g. informatics and communication; andh. peace and order.
3. Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out by OPD in accordance with their respective duties in an integrated and sustainable manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 43

1. Agencies and private parties that provide public services are required to provide lactation rooms.
2. Lactation rooms as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be proper and adequate rooms.
3. Agency heads who do not provide lactation rooms as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be subject to administrative sanctions in the form of: a. written warning; and/orb. employment sanctions based on the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
4. Private parties who do not provide lactation rooms as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be subject to administrative sanctions in the form of: a. written warning;
b. limitation of business activities;
c. suspension of business activities;
d. administrative fine; and/ore. revocation of permit.

Article 5

1. In order to improve the quality of life of women, the Regional Government is responsible for:
a. respecting and guaranteeing women's rights without discrimination in accordance with human dignity;
b. implementing Gender Mainstreaming in the Region;
c. forming a Gender Mainstreaming working group in the Region;
d. increasing Women's Empowerment in roles, quality and capacity in the fields of;
1. politics;
2. law;
3. social; and
4. economy.
e. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for Women's Empowerment in the Region;
2. The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts to improve the quality of life of women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by OPD in accordance with their duties and functions.

