3. In addition to promotive efforts as referred to in paragraph (2), Regional Government may conduct other activities in the framework of health provision.

Article 27

1. Health rehabilitation as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (3) letter b constitutes efforts that include aspects of:a. promotive, improving so that health status increasingly improves;
b. preventive, preventing from contracting disease;
c. curative, curing a person from illness physically and psychologically; d. rehabilitative, maintaining so that a person who has not fully recovered can return to being fit as before.
2. Health rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted comprehensively, accessible 24 (twenty-four) hours, with quality and conducted in accordance with health service standards.
3. Women and Children who are victims of acts of violence have the right to free health services, including:a. medical services;
b. medicolegal services;
c. psychosocial counseling; andd. Visum et Repertum.
4. Further provisions regarding the procedures for implementing health services as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 19

1. Parents in efforts to protect the rights of children play a role in:  a. caring for, maintaining, educating, and protecting children; b. developing children according to their abilities, talents, and interests;
c. providing formal and non-formal education;
d. guiding children to behave in accordance with religion, ethics and morals as well as customs; ande. preventing marriage at the age of children.
2. In the event that parents are absent, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to a certain reason, cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be transferred to the family.

5.3.text:
Article 16

1. The role of the Regional Government in efforts to Protect Women and Children:a. establishing and implementing policies, programs and activities for the Protection of Women and Children;
b. making efforts to prevent acts of violence against women including criminal acts of trafficking in persons;
c. increasing the effectiveness of services for women and Children who are victims of violence;
d. increasing efforts to protect women and Child workers;
e. strengthening and developing institutions providing Protection services for Women and Children;
f. cooperating with the Government, other Regional Governments, the private sector, the business community and/or non-governmental organizations in efforts to prevent acts of violence against women and Children;
g. involving the active role of families, ninik mamak, bundo kanduang, customary leaders, religious leaders, the Community, and institutions in the field of Protection of Women and Children;
2. The role of the Regional Government in efforts to protect women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by Regional Apparatus Organizations in accordance with their duties and functions and may coordinate with relevant agencies.

Article 20

1. In order to prevent acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government:a. conducts socialization regarding the prevention of violence against women and children by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. provides guidelines on handling acts of violence against women and children;
c. involves community participation in recovery and reintegration; d. develops an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women and children;
e. develops family empowerment programs;
f. strengthens the function of community organizations engaged in women's empowerment and child protection; andg. provides public facilities that are comfortable and safe for women and children.
2. Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out by OPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. mental and spiritual;
g. informatics and communication; andh. peace and order.
3. Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out by OPD in accordance with their respective duties in an integrated and sustainable manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 47

1. Regional Government in efforts to fulfill Children's rights is responsible for:a. establishing and implementing policies and programs/activities for the fulfillment of Children's rights;
b. respecting and guaranteeing the human rights of every Child without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, ethnicity, culture and language, legal status of the Child, birth order of the Child, and physical and/or mental condition;
c. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for improving family quality in realizing Children's rights; d. guaranteeing the Protection, care, and welfare of Children with due regard to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, or other persons legally responsible for the Child; e. providing facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of the fulfillment of Children's rights;
f. guaranteeing the right of Children to express opinions in accordance with the age and intelligence level of the Child;
g. conducting supervision and guidance in the framework of implementing the fulfillment of Children's rights;

h.cooperating with the private sector and/or NGOs in the implementation of Child Protection;

2. The responsibility of the Regional Government in Child Protection efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus Organizations in accordance with their duties and functions.

Article 3

This Regional Regulation aims to:
a. guarantee the fulfillment of the rights of women and children in the region;
b. prevent actions, decisions and policies that violate the rights of women and children;
c. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
d. protect, provide a sense of security for victims of violence;
e. provide services to victims of violence; and
f. carry out comprehensive recovery for victims of violence.

Article 54

1. Regional Government is obligated to provide comprehensive health services for Children, so that every Child obtains optimal health status from the womb.
2. Comprehensive health services as referred to in paragraph (1) include:a. promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative efforts for basic health services;
b. exemption from cost burden for malnourished children, children with disabilities, children with special needs, children infected with HIV/AIDS, child workers, child victims of narcotics abuse, alcohol, psychotropic substances and other addictive substances, children who become victims of violence, and child victims of human trafficking;
3. Regional Government, community, family and parents are obligated to ensure that Children who are born are protected from diseases that threaten survival and/or cause disability.

Article 22

In order to prevent acts of violence against women and children, parents and family:
a. protect women and children from various violations of rights in the household;
b. encourage the formation of harmonious, educative and democratic families;
c. provide good examples to children in accordance with religious teachings and customs;
d. supervise children in their activities inside or outside the home; and e. provide opportunities for children to engage in dialogue and express their opinions democratically.

